+\begin{proposition}[Dual collision after scalar extension]
+\label{prop:native-dual-collision}
+Transposition gives the morphism of complexes
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:native-dual-collision}
+\begin{aligned}
+ d^\vee_{p,\tau,w}:{}&
+ \operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C_J}(Q_J(w),\mathcal C_J)\\
+ &\longrightarrow
+ \mathcal C_J\otimes_{\mathcal B}^{\mathbb L}
+ L\delta_p^*\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal C_I}(Q_I(w),\mathcal C_I).
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+These maps preserve the connections. Their degreewise products
+over weight define a unital morphism of convolution dg algebras
+\[
+ \prod_{w\ge0}Q_J^\vee(w)
+ \longrightarrow
+ \prod_{w\ge0}E_{p,\tau}(Q_I^\vee(w)).
+\]
+For nested ordered surjections $(p,\tau):I\to J$ and
+$(q,\upsilon):J\to K$, use the concatenated order on each fibre
+of $qp$. Under the canonical identification
+$E_{q,\upsilon}E_{p,\tau}\simeq E_{qp,\upsilon\circ\tau}$,
+\[
+ d^\vee_{qp,\upsilon\circ\tau,w}
+ =E_{q,\upsilon}(d^\vee_{p,\tau,w})
+       \circ d^\vee_{q,\upsilon,w}.
+\]
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+At weight $w$, $Q_I(w)$ is graded free on finitely many state
+words and their ordered cuts. The bar-length filtration has at
+most $w$ steps. Its differential preserves the coefficient part
+and sends a state-word basis vector to shorter bar words.
+It is therefore a finite semifree module, hence perfect over
+$\mathcal C_I$; the displayed Hom computes its derived dual.
+
+For a single shifted free module, the evaluation map identifies
+the dual after scalar extension with the scalar extension of the
+dual. The identification is natural, commutes with differentials,
+and persists under finite sums, shifts, and mapping cones.
+Applying the finite semifree filtration gives
+\[
+ \operatorname{RHom}_{\mathcal C_J}
+       (E_{p,\tau}(Q_I(w)),\mathcal C_J)
+ \simeq E_{p,\tau}(Q_I^\vee(w)).
+\]
+In the fixed free models the map in
+\eqref{eq:native-dual-collision} sends $F$ to
+$F\circ\gamma_{p,\tau,w}$, followed by this identification.
+Since $\gamma$ is a degree-zero chain map,
+$d(F\gamma)=(dF)\gamma$. The connection on Hom is the
+commutator connection, so horizontality of $\gamma$ proves the
+connection assertion as well.
+
+The cut map at weights $a,b$ has target
+$Q_I(a)\otimes_{\mathcal C_I}Q_I(b)$ and source $Q_I(a+b)$.
+The coalgebra identity for $\gamma$ consequently gives
+$d^\vee(F*G)=d^\vee(F)*d^\vee(G)$ with the tensor signs.
+In weight zero the map is the identity of $\mathcal C_J$, so it
+preserves the unit. At output weight $w$, convolution uses only
+the finitely many pairs $a+b=w$. Thus these maps form algebra
+maps on every finite weight quotient and then on their inverse
+limit, represented in the stated models by degreewise products.
+The target extends coefficients separately at each weight before
+forming that product.
+
+For nested collisions, associativity of derived scalar extension
+and the identity for the coefficient maps give the displayed
+identification of the $E$ functors. The bar maps obey
+$\gamma_{qp}=\gamma_q\circ E_q(\gamma_p)$ by
+Theorem~\ref{thm:native-collision-bar}. Precomposition reverses
+this equality. Naturality of the finite-module dual identification
+then gives the stated composition formula. Every step occurs in
+a fixed weight, so the same equality holds after completion.
+\end{proof}
+
+The scalar extension is necessary even for the binary coefficient
+fibre. Take $B=\mathbb C^2$, let $C=\mathbb C$ carry the
+$B$-action through the first projection, and put $e_2=(0,1)$.
+Every $B$-linear map $f:C\to B$ satisfies
+$e_2f(1)=f(e_2\cdot1)=0$. Its image lies in the first idempotent
+summand, so it cannot send $1$ to $1_B=(1,1)$.
+There is therefore no unital $B$-linear reverse map $C\to B$.
+After extension, $C\otimes_B^{\mathbb L}B\simeq C$, and the
+weight-zero dual collision map is the identity, as required.
 
